n="BTU/hr"
N.84="label"*78x40<M>
n="BTU/hr"
N.85="summingJunction"*48x39<M>
N.86="comment"*240x7*55x77<M>
C="{\\rtf1\\ansi\\ansicpg1252\\deff0\\deflang1033{\\fonttbl{\\f0\\fnil\\fcharset0 Arial;}}
\\viewkind4\\uc1\\pard\\fs20 1Zone model, of a 2500 ft^2 house with R15 insulation in the walls.\\par
\\par
Heating:  forced air furnace, 100,000 BTU\\par
Air Changes per hour = 4, air volume in house = 2500 * 8' = 20000 f3 \\par
cfm = 80000 f3/hour = 1333 f3/min\\par
Air Conditioning = 4 Tons\\par
Cp air = .24 BTU/Lb * degF\\par
rho air = .075 Lb/f3\\par
 \\par
R value units = (f2*degF)/(Btu/hr), typical R value = R15 in a house.\\par
Heat Flow (loss) through wall Qe = deltaTemperature * surfaceArea of walls/R\\par
Units, Qe = BTU/hr = degF * f2 / ((f2 * degF)/(BTU/hr)) = BTU/hr\\par
\\par
Heating Calculations:\\par
OAT = 20degF, Tsetpoint = 70degF, Furnace ran 10 minutes then off for 20 min for a 30 minute cycle.\\par
Thermostat deadband = 2 degF, so \\par
temperatureRate = tDot  = 2degF/10 min = .0033 degF/sec\\par
and \\par
tDot = (1/C)*( (tin -tout)*mCp) -  (wallArea/R)*(tout - OAT) \\par
tin = Register temperatrue = 80degF\\par
tout = 70 deg (setpoint temperature)\\par
m*Cp = cfm*rho*Cp*60 (to get per hour)\\par
cfm = 1333 f3/min\\par
rho = .071 Lb/f3\\par
Cp = .24 Btu/ (Lb - degF)\\par
m*Cp = 1362 Btu/(hr*degF)\\par
R = 15\\par
wall area = 130' perimeter * 20' (2 stories) = 2600f2\\par
wall area/R = 2600/15 = 173.33\\par
plug in values and solve for C\\par
tDot = .0033 = ((80 - 70)*(1362) - (70-20)*(173.33) )/C\\par
C = 1501010\\par
This produces the correct pullup time (10 minutes) but the cooloff time is only 6 minutes, it should be about 3x longer (18 minutes) but I'm using it as is.  \\par
\\par
Cooling Calculations:\\par
\\par
}
"
